{"name":"Port au Prince","hex":"#006a93","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"port","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place on the coast at which ships can shelter, or dock to load and unload cargo or passengers.","A town or city containing such a place, a port city."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to port, the left-hand side of a vessel when facing the bow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/port"},{"term":"prince","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A (male) ruler, a sovereign; a king, monarch. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A female monarch."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To behave or act like a prince.","To transform (someone) into a prince."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prince"}],"entries":[{"title":"Port au Prince (1790 ship)","blocks":[{"text":"Port au Prince was built in France in 1790. The British Royal Navy captured her in 1793 off Port-au-Prince, Haiti. Her original name was General Dumourier; her new owners named her for her place of capture. She became a letter of marque, slave ship in the triangular trade in enslaved people, and privateer cum whaler. In 1806 she anchored at a Tongan island where the local inhabitants massacred most of her crew and then scuttled her."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_au_Prince_(1790_ship)","wikidata":"Q3004136"},{"title":"Port-au-Prince","blocks":[{"text":"Port-au-Prince is the capital and most populous city of Haiti. The city's population was estimated at 1,200,000 in 2022 with the metropolitan area estimated at a population of 2,618,894. The metropolitan area is defined by the IHSI as including the communes of Port-au-Prince, Delmas, Cité Soleil, Tabarre, Carrefour, and Pétion-Ville."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince.jpg/330px-US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port-au-Prince","wikidata":"Q34261"},{"title":"Port","blocks":[{"text":"A port is a maritime facility comprising one or more wharves or loading areas, where ships load and discharge cargo and passengers. Although usually situated on a sea coast or estuary, ports can also be found far inland, such as Hamburg, Manchester and Duluth; these access the sea via rivers or canals. Because of their roles as ports of entry for immigrants as well as soldiers in wartime, many port cities have experienced dramatic multi-ethnic and multicultural changes throughout their histories."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Manhattan.jpg/330px-Manhattan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port","wikidata":"Q44782"},{"title":"Prince","blocks":[{"text":"A prince is a male ruler or a male member of a monarch's or former monarch's family. Prince is also a title of nobility, often hereditary, in some European states. The female equivalent is a princess. The English word derives, via the French word prince, from the Latin noun prīnceps, from primus (first) and caput (head), meaning \"the first, foremost, the chief, most distinguished, noble ruler, prince\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Prince_William_attends_Holocaust_Memorial_Day_%2854290704867%29%28cropped_3%29.jpg/330px-Prince_William_attends_Holocaust_Memorial_Day_%2854290704867%29%28cropped_3%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince","wikidata":"Q2747456"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince_%28cropped2%29.jpg/500px-US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince_%28cropped2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince_%28cropped2%29.jpg/1280px-US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince_%28cropped2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince_%28cropped2%29.jpg/500px-US_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince_%28cropped2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUS_Navy_100221-N-5787K-002_An_aerial_view_of_the_logistical_area_near_the_port_in_Port-au-Prince_(cropped2).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/The_National_Palace_%28White_House_of_Haiti%29%2C_September_2009_1.jpg/500px-The_National_Palace_%28White_House_of_Haiti%29%2C_September_2009_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/The_National_Palace_%28White_House_of_Haiti%29%2C_September_2009_1.jpg/1280px-The_National_Palace_%28White_House_of_Haiti%29%2C_September_2009_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/The_National_Palace_%28White_House_of_Haiti%29%2C_September_2009_1.jpg/500px-The_National_Palace_%28White_House_of_Haiti%29%2C_September_2009_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipe